  "tour": {
    "title": "Romans before Romans: a map of Paul’s argument",
    "intro": "Before you start reading, this tour gives you the setting, the central claim, and a map of the letter’s four movements. Listen for the concrete problem in Rome beneath Paul’s theology: how Jews and Gentiles can become one people under Jesus.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:63bf2f5f-com-6cd6a9f3-108c-38ad-b4b8-02550ba51633",
        "startSec": 800,
        "endSec": 980,
        "why": "Begin with the real congregation Paul is addressing rather than treating Romans as an abstract theology textbook. This clip describes “Gentile groups” and “Jewish groups” that “aren’t really getting along,” then identifies Paul’s aim: that they worship “with one mind and one voice.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Backstory to Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/i5pj3w/backstory.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1197,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:d09c8ed4-f6cf-11ee-8072-b7002c006746",
        "startSec": 1400,
        "endSec": 1860,
        "why": "Now hear how Paul frames his own letter at the opening: Romans 1:1–6 functions as a “table of contents,” presenting the gospel as news about Jesus, the Davidic and Spirit-empowered king. The clip lands on “a different king who can include diverse people in his kingdom,” giving you the letter’s political and theological frame.",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans Overview: Jesus is King",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG3092543348.mp3?updated=1712708592",
        "durationSec": 2862,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6bbfa166-com-20db375c-a035-35f2-8b38-516fd9b58eb7",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 820,
        "why": "Use this as your chapter-by-chapter map. The speaker names “four big chunks”—chapters 1–4, 5–8, 9–11, and 12–16—and walks through how universal need leads to righteousness through Christ, new life by the Spirit, the question of Israel, and a united community.",
        "episodeTitle": "An Overview of the Letter to the Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/x82wrk/overview.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1242,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6bbfa166-com-20db375c-a035-35f2-8b38-516fd9b58eb7",
        "startSec": 870,
        "endSec": 1010,
        "why": "Finish by adjusting the lens you bring to the book. The speaker cautions against reading Romans as “almost exclusively about our own individual personal salvation” and argues that the letter’s ending keeps the “people of God” and their shared worship at the center. That helps chapters 9–11 and the practical material in 12–16 belong to the same argument.",
        "episodeTitle": "An Overview of the Letter to the Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/x82wrk/overview.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1242,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "As you read, keep the four movements in view: Paul levels Jew and Gentile under sin and announces God’s righteousness through faith; explains the new life Christ and the Spirit create; wrestles with God’s faithfulness to Israel; and finally shows what this one family must look like in practice. Romans is not only answering how individuals are saved—it is arguing what kind of people the gospel makes, and how they must live together.",
    "totalSeconds": 1600
  },
